/**
 * Day 98 — Phase 5 item 7: globe refinement of the existing countries, China first.
 *
 * Before this, China before 1815 was drawn only by the schematic rings of the Shang, Zhou, Qin, Han,
 * Tang, Song, Mongol, Ming and Qing entities (and the Tibetan, Nanzhao and steppe rings at the
 * edges). Their keyframes left China empty for centuries — 120–620, 870–990, 1220–1240, 1320–1370
 * and 1570–1660 — and the divided eras were drawn as one block or not at all. This pack adds the
 * states of the divided eras, from the Warring States (475 BCE) to the Kingdom of Tungning (to 1683),
 * and redraws the Zhou, Song, Mongol (Yuan) and Qing rings from the same pieces where those states
 * stand beside them (see the fixes). The nations layer draws China (id `china`, from 1815/1914);
 * this pack uses other ids and has no keyframe after 1813.
 *
 * How it is built (docs/DATA.md, "Country-pack globe backfill"):
 *  - 46 pieces from Natural Earth 1:10m admin-1 (public domain): Chinese provinces, some split along
 *    lines of latitude or longitude (Hebei at 38.9°N, Shanxi at 36°N and 39°N, Henan into the north,
 *    Luoyang, Xinzheng, Kaifeng and the south, Shaanxi at 33.6°N and 35.8°N, Gansu at 103°E, Inner
 *    Mongolia at 108°E and 116°E, Liaoning at 122°E, Jiangsu and Anhui along the Huai and the
 *    Yangtze, Hubei into the Gorges, Xiangyang, Jiangling and Wuchang), and Taiwan. Xinjiang, Tibet,
 *    Qinghai, Hainan, western Sichuan and the far north are not cut. Generator: et-tools day98-pieces.py.
 *  - A polity is a dated list of pieces; every change is a one-year step.
 *  - Every polity has a certainty level and sources, and links the China timeline items.
 * Not drawn (timeline items and pins only): the Spring and Autumn states and the small Warring States
 * (Lu, Song, Zheng, Wey, Zhongshan); Gongsun Yuan’s Liaodong; the Former Liang, Western Yan, Southern
 * Yan, Xia and Northern Liang among the Sixteen Kingdoms; Goguryeo’s Liaodong (c. 404–668); the
 * Western Shu (405–413); Li Keyong’s Jin, the Yan of Liu Shouguang, the Wuping and Qingyuan regimes;
 * the Tangut Dingnan governors before 1038; Eastern Xia in Manchuria (1215–1233); Xu Shouhui’s
 * Tianwan, Han Lin’er’s Song, the Ming Xia and Fang Guozhen among the Yuan rebels; the Shun and Xi of
 * Li Zicheng and Zhang Xianzhong; Geng Jingzhong and Shang Zhixin among the Three Feudatories; Dutch
 * and Spanish Formosa; the Xiongnu, Xianbei, Rouran and other steppe empires outside the pieces.
 */
